AUDIT RECEIPT: THUMB PC ADDRESS, STACK ADDRESS, LITERAL WORD LOAD.

Source branch: fp-91-english-armv7-t32-execution-contract
Work envelope: https://github.com/isomorphisms/flexible-pipes/issues/91
Review: https://github.com/dilapidated-shed/English-major-C/pull/3

Status: observed diagnostic assembly and disassembly, independently
recalculated bitfields, GitHub source publication.
NOT verified physical ARM execution.
NOT an accepted English Major C compiler, parser or interpreter.
NOT independent AICI acceptance or an authorized merge.

I. NORMATIVE SOURCE.

Arm Limited, ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A and ARMv7-R
edition, ARM DDI 0406C.d / ID040418, especially the A6
16-bit Thumb decode tables and the A8 instruction descriptions
for ADR T1, ADD (SP plus immediate) T1 and LDR (literal) T1.
The A4 unified-assembler address rules specify that a Thumb
PC read is current instruction address +4, and Align(PC,4)
clears the two lowest bits before computing some literal
or PC-relative addresses.

Source identification:
https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0406/cd
Additional non-normative A-profile T32 instruction description:
https://mikhailarkhipov.github.io/ARM-doc/A32/adr.html

II. IDENTIFIED DIAGNOSTIC HOST AND TOOLS.

Host: Linux x86-64 local disposable analysis environment.
Compiler: clang 17.0.0, Swift/LLVM revision
10999b6d034fe318f3d56c83bddb6572593a8bb0.
Decoder: llvm-objdump 17.0.0.
Target: armv7a-none-eabi, Thumb state, ELF32 little-endian ARM.

The diagnostic tool binary file digests, dynamic library closure,
compiler/linker runtime and target CPU were not fully captured.
Thus these source/object/output SHA256 values describe a concrete
temporary observation, not a complete reproducible ICK or NDK build.

III. ALL 4096 A000..AFFF HALFWORDS.

A transient assembler source placed all 4096 literal .hword
values A000 through AFFF in increasing numerical order into
a Thumb .text section. Each group of sixteen consecutive
values was written on a .hword directive line.

Temporary source SHA256:
80280a8596c41854afcbcda639b2fc1397a68723096e8539976dc01b1fa5cdcc
Temporary ELF32 ARM object SHA256:
b8e7da5ce1a16e63f70215d2ca8835b9096b733376dbde3a17ee88093f57c543
Temporary LLVM disassembly SHA256:
9d6caf045875ba5ba79f8312ff7ceb94c6eaa74905fa83ac88bf9ec11f48f392

Decoded instruction totals:
  2048 T1 ADR instruction halfwords, A000–A7FF;
  2048 T1 ADD Rd,SP,#immediate halfwords, A800–AFFF;
  zero unexplained alternate or missing forms.

A separate arithmetic comparison checked all 4096
offset/halfword/low-register/immediate combinations:
  current diagnostic section offset = 2*(halfword-A000);
  destination = bits 10..8, R0 through R7;
  byte increment = 4 * unsigned bits 7..0.
The decoder name/operands/offsets agreed with every
row and exactly matched the two 2048-entry classes.

The associated 4096-row committed source is:
fixtures/armv7-a-t32-address-generation-halfwords.tsv

The arithmetic examples in its two result columns
are NOT actual runtime addresses, but illustrative
assumptions: instruction begins at 0x1000 or 0x1002,
and SP is assumed equal to 0x2000.
PC base for both instruction addresses is 0x1004,
because Align(0x1004,4)=Align(0x1006,4)=0x1004.
This is not a claim that a physical ARM program's
instructions were loaded at those virtual addresses.

IV. ALL 2048 LITERAL WORD LOAD HALFWORDS.

A separate diagnostic generated .hword 0x4800 through
.hword 0x4FFF, strictly in numerical order.

Temporary source SHA256:
fe022a60245b0bf237182c995626d44d592439b654b642fa869dc35574d56eda
Temporary ELF32 ARM object SHA256:
e9bcb95af24d097452bbe99cf07d257d56865088653e64f73af06615e5b399f2
Temporary LLVM disassembly SHA256:
3b2c641d3d6e8017281579c8465d4fe5a2b1ea242318e8c459f785dc8a3702e0

All 2048 entries decoded as two-byte T1
LDR literal instructions with destination R0..R7,
unsigned imm8*4 byte offsets, and PC-relative
word addresses.

The first diagnostic comparison script FAILED
every one of the 2048 text comparisons. The
comparison expected decimal text after "#",
while LLVM printed hexadecimal strings
with prefix "#0x". This was a diagnostic
representation error, not evidence that
ARM immediate fields differ from the model.

The independent comparison was repaired to
parse the printed numeric literal using its
actual indicated radix, rather than assuming
a printed representation. The renewed
comparison checked every raw halfword,
destination, scaled immediate, and
instruction byte offset; all 2048 matched,
with zero remaining discrepancies.

The complete committed data enumeration is:
fixtures/armv7-a-t32-pc-relative-word-load-halfwords.tsv

The diagnostic disassembler did NOT actually
perform those 2048 data memory reads.
Any successful LDR runtime state additionally
depends on target memory mappings, permissions,
endianness, conditions and faults.

V. A REAL SIXTEEN-BYTE CODE/DATA AMBIGUITY.

The diagnostic assembly source combined T32
.hword instruction encodings and a literal
.word 0x12345678 in the SAME .text section.
It had exact raw bytes:
00 A0 01 49 02 E0 00 BE 78 56 34 12 00 BF 01 DF

SHA256 diagnostic source:
7bcf3e392d1dc62a9aece9425abfc198509509e9acaca4d6cdb2b3a93b71b3ad
SHA256 diagnostic ELF32 object:
e5627dbcebaffeed79ccb3851d0e6b7533b1e2d8f6fb8eb5b6a79d063895d10b
SHA256 diagnostic linear disassembly:
c89da3af7a3c63df506f5958b89f4e169142de9b5422bcca2f6ad986415febd4
SHA256 diagnostic raw section dump:
1f619f98b570557f15d4b4b1924b9728bfaa08d30842c2347a8eeb510e73225a

The four literal bytes are at .text section
offset 8..11. A naive linear disassembler
displayed those bytes as two perfectly
plausible Thumb instructions:
  halfword 0x5678 -> LDRSB R0,[R7,R1]
  halfword 0x1234 -> ASRS R4,R6,#8.
But the ORIGINAL assembler source specified
those bytes as a SINGLE DATA WORD,
and the actual B instruction at offset
four branches directly to offset twelve.
Thus a control-flow trace beginning at offset
zero neither executes the breakpoint at offset
six nor executes those literal bytes as code.

A separate temporary arithmetic state model
assumed the machine section was loaded at
virtual address 0x1000, had T32 executable
entries at the intended offsets, and
could read the four literal data bytes
with little-endian semantics.

It produced the ordered index trace:
  0x1000 ADR R0, Align(PC,4) + 0;
  0x1002 LDR R1, [Align(PC,4)+4];
  0x1004 B with positive four-byte displacement;
  0x100C NOP;
  0x100E STOP before ungranted SVC.
Known results before SVC:
  R0=0x1004;
  R1=0x12345678;
  other undeclared register values UNKNOWN;
  N/Z/C/V unchanged from UNKNOWN starting flags;
  ordinary data memory unchanged.

The arithmetic check verified the five visited
instruction-boundary addresses and two computed
register values. No physical CPU performed this trace.
The assumption that the .text mapping permits
ordinary data reads must be tested independently
on any future concrete target.

VI. MEANING AND EXPLICIT LIMITS.

ADR constructs an address numerical value.
SP-relative ADD constructs another numerical
value without changing SP.
LDR literal constructs a PC-relative address
AND then reads a word from it.
B changes the execution index.
The existence of a register value numerically
equal to an executable address does not branch
there. Decodable instruction-looking DATA bytes
do not automatically become executed instructions.

Existing long operation 40 is narrowed to exact
ADR T1 encoding.
Existing long operation 41 is narrowed to exact
LDR literal T1 encoding.
New long operation 73 separately names
SP-relative ADD T1, where destination is R0-R7
and SP itself remains unchanged.

All three operation families preserve comparison
flags and have two-byte instruction encodings.
Their complete runtime semantics in IT blocks
still require independent full ITSTATE acceptance.

VII. EXPLICITLY OPEN VERIFICATION OBLIGATIONS.

The source files do not demonstrate:
  - all ARMv7-A A32 and T32 encodings;
  - full ARMv7-A user-mode memory attributes;
  - target-specific executable/readable permissions;
  - ARMv7 cache, speculation, or real DRAM transfers;
  - data aborts under varied memory mappings;
  - independent hostile test acceptance in AICI;
  - parser/typechecker/lowering or an executable
    English Major C semantic runtime;
  - ICK or Android NDK maintained build qualification;
  - a successful hardware execution of the example;
  - completion of the R lm()/QR disassembly translation.

This is source-level architectural work,
real foreign diagnostic decode evidence,
and separately computed example arithmetic.
It is NOT finished compiler or processor qualification.

